th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-OR-Modelling/unbounded-linear-program.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-OR-Modelling/unbounded-linear-program.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_unbounded_linear_program_7f2dc898_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\max\\ 2x_1+x_2$ subject to $x_1-x_2\\le4$ (slack $s_1$), $x_1\\le6$ (slack $s_2$) and $x_1,x_2\\ge0$, two pivots give the feasible tableau $z-s_1+3s_2=14$, $x_1+s_2=6$ (basic $x_1$), $x_2-s_1+s_2=2$ (basic $x_2$). On the feasible points obtained with $s_2=0$ and $s_1=10$, what is the value of $2x_1+x_2$?","options":["$14$","$24$","$34$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"208093cd9304ebecc3683211","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_unbounded_linear_program_7f2dc898_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\min\\ x_1-2x_2$ subject to $-x_1+x_2\\le3$ and $x_1,x_2\\ge0$, consider the feasible points $(x_1,x_2)=(t,\\,t+3)$ with $t\\ge0$. What is the objective value at these points?","options":["$-t-6$","$-t+6$","$t-6$","$-3t-6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a63d1f30570c3a62f8d5d301","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_unbounded_linear_program_7f2dc898_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a feasible maximization tableau $z+\\sum_j q_jx_j=z_0$, the nonbasic $x_4$ has $q_4=2$ and only negative entries in the constraint rows. What follows from this column alone?","options":["The objective is unbounded, since $x_4$ can rise freely","Nothing about unboundedness, since $x_4$ is not improving","The tableau has no feasible solution, since all entries are negative","The objective is unbounded below, since $z$ falls as $x_4$ rises"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d26c0be9e6b28647d6895d7d","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_unbounded_linear_program_7f2dc898_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A maximization LP has a nonempty feasible region in which every variable stays below some fixed number. Can its objective be unbounded?","options":["Yes: a region with infinitely many points allows endless growth","Yes: if some objective coefficient is larger than all the others","No: a bounded region always has exactly one optimal corner point","No: with bounded variables a linear objective is bounded too"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ce8256fd53d0025f7ca6f323","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}